# Model delegation - ring-fencing top-tier tokens

Claude Code twin: `.claude/rules/model-delegation.md` (canonical - includes the proven token
ratio and the full skeleton brief). Claude Code sessions have `builder` and `recon` subagents
pinned to Sonnet in `.claude/agents/`.

## The split (applies to Cursor model choice too)

- **Opus 5 - the default top tier**: architecture decisions, writing briefs/specs, seam +
  security review, debugging weird failures, deploy go/no-go. Not typing implementations.
- **Fable 5.1 - named exception only**: genuinely frontier reasoning, or debugging that already
  defeated Opus 5. $10/$50 per MTok vs Opus 5's $5/$25 (checked 2026-09-12) - 2x on output and
  cache writes, but NOT on every line item: Fable 5.1 cache reads are $0.25/MTok against Opus 5's
  $0.50, so on a long cache-read-heavy session its input is the cheaper one. Quote the line item,
  not "twice the price". Reach for it by naming the reason, never by habit. If the reason is
  "this is hard", try higher effort on Opus 5 first.
- **Sonnet 5 - mid tier**: implementation from a self-contained brief, fan-out recon,
  docs from a spec. Runs its own verification before reporting done.
- **Haiku 4.5 / small models**: mechanical transforms only - below the bar for this repo's
  conventions. Haiku 4.5 also rejects the effort setting outright and holds 200K context, not 1M.

## Effort before tier

Raise or lower the effort setting before switching models - it is cheaper than a tier switch, and
it is the only one of the two with a cache-free form. **The default is `high`**, so "tune the
effort" is as often down as up. Low for recon and mechanical work, high/xhigh for coding and
agentic work, max only when correctness beats cost. Effort cannot fix a price tier, and does not
exist at all on Haiku 4.5.

Note a top-level effort change mid-session DOES invalidate the messages cache. The per-message
form does not - available on Opus 5 and Fable 5.1, not on Sonnet 5.

## Protect the prompt cache

Cache reads cost ~0.1x base input; cache writes cost 1.25x-2x. Continue sessions rather than
restarting them, do not switch models mid-session (caches are model-scoped), and keep the early
part of the prompt frozen - editing a rules file mid-session invalidates everything after it.
On a top-tier model, wasted cache writes are routinely the largest line on the bill.

**Default an architect session to Opus 5 and tune effort; default delegated work to Sonnet.** This
reverses the earlier "default to Sonnet, escalate when needed" advice, which fought the paragraph
above it: you discover you need to escalate MID-session (that is what "stuck debugging" means),
and switching then discards the prefix you already paid to write. Picking the tier when you OPEN
the session costs nothing, because no cache exists yet.

The repo still pins Sonnet in `.claude/settings.json`, and that is not a contradiction: a pin is a
DEFAULT, not a lock. Choosing at session start overrides it for free. The pin governs the sessions
nobody thought about - which skew trivial and belong on the cheap tier - while this rule governs
the ones you did.

Discovered mid-session that you need more? In order: raise the effort (per-message form on Opus 5
costs no cache), then delegate to a subagent or a separate session on the tier you need (its
context is its own, so your prefix survives), and only then switch the session model - saying out
loud that you did, because escalation is sticky and quietly stays on for the next twenty easy
tasks.

## Brief anatomy (all six, every delegation - human or agent)

1. Context (3-5 sentences, incl. parallel workstreams)
2. Read-first list - exact files AND line ranges, nothing open-ended
3. FIXED contracts between workstreams, marked "do not renegotiate"
4. Deliverables with acceptance criteria
5. Verify-before-finishing commands (tests, type-check, ASCII-dash grep)
6. Report cap + explicit don'ts (no commit/deploy/DB writes unless stated)

## Review discipline

Review seams, not surfaces: grep the contract joints (header names, param shapes, grants)
instead of re-reading whole files. Read deviation notes first - they carry the real-world facts
the brief got wrong. Keep parallel workstreams in disjoint directories.

## Never economize on

Security-adjacent changes, anything crossing more than two components, deploy decisions, and
debugging where the symptom lies.
